## Abstract
Clinical reasoning benchmarks are increasingly used to compare foundation models and to motivate clinical deployments. Yet evaluations are often reported under a single prompt, implicitly treating wording as a nuisance variable. Here we quantify prompt sensitivity in medical multiple-choice question answering (MCQ) evaluation and test the limits of a fixed “magic prompt” injection. Across {{ key_results.core_models_n }} frontier models and semantically aligned prompt paraphrases, the same questions yield large within-model accuracy dispersion (median within-model range: {{ key_results.prompt_range_median }} percentage points; MedXpertQA subset). We then select a short injection on a disjoint dev set and evaluate it on held-out test items from three medical QA datasets (n={{ key_results.n_questions }} questions; {{ key_results.core_models_n }} models), finding heterogeneous effects and an overall mean accuracy change of {{ key_results.magic_gain_mean }} percentage points (95% paired bootstrap CI {{ key_results.magic_gain_ci_low }} to {{ key_results.magic_gain_ci_high }}). Overall, prompt wording and prompt-level interventions can materially change measured medical QA capability and should be validated across prompts, datasets, and models before deployment.

## Main Text

### Introduction
Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly adopted for clinical question answering and reasoning benchmarks. However, medical evaluations often report point estimates under a single prompt, implicitly assuming that prompt phrasing is a minor nuisance variable. In high-stakes settings, this assumption is risky: small instruction changes can reshape the model’s behavior, potentially altering diagnosis and management choices. We test whether (i) meaning-preserving prompt paraphrases systematically change MCQ accuracy under deterministic decoding, and (ii) a fixed, short injection prompt can robustly improve performance across datasets and models.

### Results

#### Prompt sensitivity in medical QA (MedPrompt Effect)
Across the core model panel ({{ key_results.core_models_n }} models) and datasets, we observe clear prompt sensitivity even with deterministic decoding. On the MedXpertQA subset, within-model accuracy varied across paraphrased prompts with a median range of {{ key_results.prompt_range_median }} percentage points (IQR {{ key_results.prompt_range_iqr_low }}–{{ key_results.prompt_range_iqr_high }}).

